1
00:00:05,271 --> 00:00:08,027
On the morning of 5 August 1962,

2
00:00:08,238 --> 00:00:11,195
Marilyn Monroe was found dead
at her home in Los Angeles.

3
00:00:11,546 --> 00:00:12,519
She was 36.

4
00:00:14,251 --> 00:00:17,712
Within three days, the world had
read the first newspaper reports,

5
00:00:18,064 --> 00:00:19,559
and her funeral had been held.

6
00:00:21,151 --> 00:00:24,252
This is her story, told two
ways: in her own voice,

7
00:00:24,443 --> 00:00:26,279
and in the pages that
printed the news.

8
00:00:32,691 --> 00:00:36,971
The Associated Press introduced
her in 1962 as Norma Jean Baker,

9
00:00:37,282 --> 00:00:40,579
the frightened waif who became the
movies' famous Marilyn Monroe.

10
00:00:42,090 --> 00:00:44,984
In a Life magazine interview,
published just before she died,

11
00:00:45,275 --> 00:00:47,748
she remembered a foster family
sending her to the movies,

12
00:00:47,899 --> 00:00:48,950
to get her out of the house.

13
00:00:49,501 --> 00:00:51,133
She sat up front, she said,

14
00:00:51,564 --> 00:00:55,779
"a little kid all alone." And,
in her words, "I loved it."

15
00:00:57,371 --> 00:01:00,829
Darryl Zanuck of Twentieth
Century-Fox saw a screen test of her,

16
00:01:01,039 --> 00:01:03,174
after two other studios
had turned her down.

17
00:01:03,766 --> 00:01:05,199
He signed her to a contract.

18
00:01:06,731 --> 00:01:11,411
On 13 September 1953, she appeared
on The Jack Benny Program.

19
00:01:11,963 --> 00:01:14,779
Here is her own voice,
from that broadcast.

20
00:01:44,020 --> 00:01:47,976
Five months later, in February
1954, she flew to Korea.

21
00:01:48,567 --> 00:01:51,071
The United States Marine
Corps estimated that she sang

22
00:01:51,091 --> 00:01:54,105
for thirteen thousand men
of the First Marine Division.

23
00:01:54,656 --> 00:01:57,070
An Army film shows her
arriving by helicopter.

24
00:02:04,581 --> 00:02:09,720
Her best-known song came on 19
May 1962, at Madison Square Garden.

25
00:02:10,212 --> 00:02:12,095
Less than three months
before she died,

26
00:02:12,115 --> 00:02:14,510
she sang Happy Birthday
to President Kennedy.

27
00:02:53,221 --> 00:02:55,917
Saturday, 4 August 1962.

28
00:02:56,408 --> 00:02:59,404
What is known about that day comes
from press reports at the time,

29
00:02:59,515 --> 00:03:01,609
and those reports did
not always agree.

30
00:03:03,080 --> 00:03:04,262
Her psychiatrist, Dr.

31
00:03:04,282 --> 00:03:07,878
Ralph Greenson, said she called
him at 5:15 in the afternoon,

32
00:03:08,189 --> 00:03:09,922
upset and looking for reassurance.

33
00:03:10,192 --> 00:03:11,224
They talked for an hour.

34
00:03:11,595 --> 00:03:13,488
He told her to take a drive to relax.

35
00:03:13,838 --> 00:03:14,509
She did not.

36
00:03:16,001 --> 00:03:19,743
Her housekeeper, Eunice Murray, said
that she went to her room at eight,

37
00:03:19,934 --> 00:03:21,428
and seemed to be in good spirits.

38
00:03:23,061 --> 00:03:24,032
Two days later,

39
00:03:24,123 --> 00:03:25,586
the Associated Press reported

40
00:03:25,606 --> 00:03:28,292
that actor Peter Lawford
had phoned around seven,

41
00:03:28,312 --> 00:03:29,424
to invite her to dinner.

42
00:03:29,675 --> 00:03:31,749
She declined, saying she was tired.

43
00:03:33,181 --> 00:03:37,081
Joe DiMaggio Junior called
later, the report said.

44
00:03:37,372 --> 00:03:39,889
And then came another call,
from a person unknown.

45
00:03:41,421 --> 00:03:42,293
On the first day,

46
00:03:42,403 --> 00:03:46,202
the same wire service had reported no
long-distance calls from her number.

47
00:03:46,653 --> 00:03:49,109
Neither report says
who the last caller was.

48
00:03:55,661 --> 00:03:57,576
Early on Sunday, 5 August,

49
00:03:57,747 --> 00:04:01,629
she was found dead at her home in
Brentwood, a district of Los Angeles.

50
00:04:03,081 --> 00:04:07,040
By Monday, the news was on the front
page of the Washington Evening Star:

51
00:04:07,412 --> 00:04:10,309
Death of Marilyn Monroe
Ends Turbulent Career.

52
00:04:11,961 --> 00:04:14,879
A coroner's special "suicide
team" was already at work.

53
00:04:15,391 --> 00:04:18,429
The paper said that the
evidence so far was conflicting.

54
00:04:19,981 --> 00:04:23,195
Another page printed her own
words, from that Life interview,

55
00:04:23,326 --> 00:04:27,843
under the headline "Never Used
to Being Happy." She had said:

56
00:04:28,174 --> 00:04:29,786
"I was never used to being happy,

57
00:04:29,936 --> 00:04:32,130
so that wasn't something
I ever took for granted."

58
00:04:33,520 --> 00:04:35,273
On Wednesday, 8 August,

59
00:04:35,664 --> 00:04:39,180
about fifteen people were invited
to her funeral service in Westwood.

60
00:04:39,671 --> 00:04:41,944
Police prepared for thousands of fans.

61
00:04:42,756 --> 00:04:45,290
By midnight, a hundred
waited outside.

62
00:04:46,660 --> 00:04:49,915
The eulogy was given by her
drama coach, Lee Strasberg.

63
00:04:50,306 --> 00:04:54,582
The typed text says: "Marilyn
Monroe was a legend." And then:

64
00:04:54,953 --> 00:04:56,685
"I did not know this Marilyn Monroe.

65
00:04:57,156 --> 00:04:59,870
We, gathered here today,
knew only Marilyn."

66
00:05:06,301 --> 00:05:07,133
On the first day,

67
00:05:07,223 --> 00:05:10,743
the Associated Press had asked
two questions: "Was it suicide?

68
00:05:10,834 --> 00:05:12,729
Had she called someone
before she died?"

69
00:05:14,301 --> 00:05:17,577
In those same days, rumors
moved faster than facts.

70
00:05:18,128 --> 00:05:21,304
A report from Mexico City
blamed an unhappy romance.

71
00:05:21,635 --> 00:05:23,990
Her representatives
denied it emphatically.

72
00:05:25,301 --> 00:05:29,392
On 17 August, the Los Angeles
coroner, Theodore Curphey,

73
00:05:29,412 --> 00:05:32,149
announced that he considered
her death a probable suicide.

74
00:05:33,640 --> 00:05:34,803
In 1982,

75
00:05:34,823 --> 00:05:38,139
the Los Angeles County District
Attorney reviewed the case again.

76
00:05:38,710 --> 00:05:41,766
The review found no evidence
of murder or conspiracy,

77
00:05:41,856 --> 00:05:43,950
and said the original
finding still stood.

78
00:05:45,381 --> 00:05:48,395
One witness from her last
months is still on record.

79
00:05:48,986 --> 00:05:51,080
The photographer Lawrence
Schiller was with her

80
00:05:51,130 --> 00:05:52,722
on the set of her final film.

81
00:05:53,313 --> 00:05:57,089
In 2012, he told Fox News
about the day she turned 36.

82
00:06:03,951 --> 00:06:06,670
The studio fired her, and
the film was abandoned.

83
00:06:07,262 --> 00:06:09,559
Two months after that
birthday, she was dead.

84
00:06:17,951 --> 00:06:21,652
The first newspapers could only say
that the evidence was conflicting.

85
00:06:21,984 --> 00:06:23,819
Decades of theories came after.

86
00:06:25,211 --> 00:06:27,311
Which Marilyn do you
remember: the legend,

87
00:06:27,442 --> 00:06:28,899
or the person her friends knew?
